window.SEARCH_INDEX = [{"t":"The Qtile Dotfiles manual","p":"Home","u":"index.html","b":"A complete Arch Linux desktop — tiling window manager, 22 themes that retint every application at once, a drop-stash, a package picker and a boot splash with your own name on it. One command installs all of it. Read this first if you have just installed. There is no display manager and no autologin. When the machine reboots you land on a black text console with a blinking cursor — that is the install working, not failing. Log in with the username and password you chose, then type letsgo (or startx ) to start the desktop. See Your first login ."},{"t":"What this is","p":"Home","u":"index.html#what","b":"This is a dotfiles repository that installs an entire desktop on a clean Arch Linux base system: qtile under X11, a themed bar, a set of keyboard-driven popups, about fifty command-line tools, and the package declarations that put all of it there. It is not a distribution and not a desktop environment — it is one person's desktop, written down completely enough that a second machine can reproduce it. Two halves, and it is worth knowing which one you are standing on: Half Portable? Why The configs and tools — everything under .config/ Any Linux Nothing is hardcoded to a distro, username, monito"},{"t":"What it looks like","p":"Home","u":"index.html#look","b":"Tiling · launcher · qdrop · qupdate · a theme switch carried by the restart veil. 10 of the 22 modes. The full list, and what each switch retints, is on The themes ."},{"t":"The two ways to install","p":"Home","u":"index.html#install","b":"From the ATI-OS USB → A bootable installation medium with the expensive compiles already done. Answer seven questions; the desktop builds itself on the first boot. Roughly 20 minutes of unattended work instead of two hours. From a git clone → You already have Arch installed. Clone the repo and run one script; 46 modules run end to end with no questions asked. Set aside an hour or two and about 25 GB. Both routes end at the same desktop. The USB is faster because pacman-static (an hour on its own), espanso, paru and whisper.cpp ship prebuilt rather than being compiled on your machine."},{"t":"Your first login","p":"Home","u":"index.html#firstlogin","b":"The install deliberately leaves you at a text console. The wizard's disable-dm module disables every display manager it can find ( lightdm , gdm , sddm , lxdm ); nothing starts X for you and nothing logs you in. Log in at the TTY with the username and password you chose during the install. Type letsgo and press Enter. If that is not found, type startx . Why two commands. letsgo is a fish function , not a shell alias, so it exists only when the account's login shell is fish. The wizard's login-shell module runs chsh to fish — but only if fish installed successfully, and it skips itself with a m"},{"t":"Requirements","p":"Home","u":"index.html#requirements","b":"Arch Linux , clean base install X11 only — Wayland is not supported No display manager — TTY plus letsgo / startx Packages are managed declaratively via dcli x86_64 only. The GPU module detects Intel, AMD or NVIDIA from the PCI vendor id and installs the matching driver set plus the right CPU microcode. ARM (aarch64) is not supported : the module detects a non-x86_64 architecture and skips PCI and microcode detection rather than installing something wrong, but several AUR packages this repo depends on ( picom-ftlabs-git , qtile-extras , brave-bin , google-chrome ) are not all built for ARM, so"},{"t":"What is not tested","p":"Home","u":"index.html#honest","b":"The repository is explicit about this and so is this manual. What is verified, on a clean Arch VM: 46 of 46 modules install, validate.sh passes inside the guest, every boot entry's root= matches the guest's real root device, and qtile starts and loads this config rather than silently falling back to its built-in one. On real hardware, additionally: the boot splash, and picom's glx backend with the window animations on Mesa Intel HD 520. Not verified, because nobody here has the hardware: AMD and NVIDIA graphics. graphics-amd.yaml is reasoned from pacman -Si , never observed on AMD hardware. A "},{"t":"Where to go next","p":"Home","u":"index.html#next","b":"Keybindings → Every binding in the config, grouped and in tables. Also available live: press Super + Shift + K then k . The themes → 22 modes, how a switch propagates, and the full list of what retints. Widgets and popups → The bar, the logo menu, the qtile popups and the eww onboarding tour. Everyday tools → qdrop, qupdate, the theme picker, the rofi menus, phone mirroring and voice dictation. Troubleshooting → The common failures first, each with the symptom you would actually search for. Reference → File layout, where config lives, how stow works here, and the test layers. Based on Distrotu"},{"t":"Install from the ATI-OS USB","p":"Install from the ATI-OS USB","u":"install-usb.html","b":"A bootable installation medium that installs Arch, this configuration and 31 already-compiled AUR packages onto a blank machine. Roughly 20 minutes of unattended work instead of two hours. Nothing logs you in and nothing starts X. After the install and the first boot, the machine stops at a text console. That is intended. See Logging in ."},{"t":"What ATI-OS is","p":"Install from the ATI-OS USB","u":"install-usb.html#what","b":"It lives in installScripts/iso/ . The profile is a fork of Arch's own releng archiso profile, kept deliberately close to it — every line that differs has to be re-reconciled when archiso changes, so the diff is branding, the installer, and nothing else. The saving comes from one thing: pacman-static , espanso, paru and whisper.cpp are compiled once when the ISO is built, not on every machine that installs it. pacman-static alone is about an hour, because it compiles pacman and every dependency statically from source, much of it single-threaded through autotools. File What it does aur-repo.sh B"},{"t":"Building the medium","p":"Install from the ATI-OS USB","u":"install-usb.html#build","b":"cd installScripts/iso ./aur-repo.sh # 2-3 h, cached. 31 AUR packages -> a local pacman repo ./build-iso.sh # 20-40 min. stages the profile, runs mkarchiso ./test-iso.sh # 1-2 h. boots it in qemu, installs, asserts Each takes --check to run only its preflight and touch nothing. Do that first — the preflights are the reason a failure costs seconds instead of hours. aur-repo.sh is separate from build-iso.sh on purpose: rebuilding 31 AUR packages to change a boot-menu label would be absurd, so its output is cached and build-iso.sh only checks for it. Everything is written under $ATI_ISO_WORK (defa"},{"t":"Writing it to a stick","p":"Install from the ATI-OS USB","u":"install-usb.html#write","b":"lsblk # find the right device FIRST sudo dd if=~/ati-os-build/out/ati-os-*.iso of=/dev/sdX bs=4M status=progress oflag=sync dd does not ask. Check lsblk twice. The oflag=sync matters: without it dd returns before the data is actually on the stick."},{"t":"Starting the installer","p":"Install from the ATI-OS USB","u":"install-usb.html#run","b":"Boot the stick, then at the live shell: ati-os-install It must run as root. Two other modes exist: Flag Effect --check Runs the preflight and exits. Changes nothing. --unattended Takes every answer from environment variables ( ATI_DISK , ATI_USER , ATI_PASS , ATI_HOSTNAME , ATI_TIMEZONE , ATI_KEYMAP , ATI_LOCALE , ATI_ENCRYPT , ATI_LUKS_PASS , ATI_DUALBOOT ). This is what test-iso.sh drives. It erases the named disk without confirming."},{"t":"Stage 1: preflight","p":"Install from the ATI-OS USB","u":"install-usb.html#preflight","b":"Before anything is touched, the installer checks six things and refuses if any of the fatal ones fail. Each refusal names the value that failed, rather than saying \"requirements not met\". Check Fatal? Notes UEFI firmware ( /sys/firmware/efi/efivars present) Yes \"this machine booted in BIOS/legacy mode\" . The live environment still boots on BIOS machines for rescue work — only installing is refused. Secure Boot No, but warns loudly ATI-OS installs an unsigned bootloader. With Secure Boot on, the install finishes and the machine then refuses to boot with \"no bootable device\", which looks like a "},{"t":"The medium's age","p":"Install from the ATI-OS USB","u":"install-usb.html#age","b":"The preflight reads a BUILD_EPOCH stamp baked into the ISO and decides whether the prebuilt AUR packages can still be trusted. Everything else on an ISO ages harmlessly because it is refetched from mirrors; these binaries were compiled against one particular build day's libraries. Age What happens ≤ 180 days Used. \"prebuilt packages are current enough\" 181–365 days Used, with a warning that a rebuilt ISO is the safer answer > 365 days Not used. The AUR set is compiled from source instead — correct, and roughly two hours rather than twenty minutes. The disk floor also rises from 30 GB to 60 GB,"},{"t":"The questions","p":"Install from the ATI-OS USB","u":"install-usb.html#questions","b":"These are the real prompts, in the order the installer asks them. Defaults are shown in brackets; pressing Enter accepts them."},{"t":"1. The target disk","p":"Install from the ATI-OS USB","u":"install-usb.html#q-disk","b":"── target disk ── /dev/sda 238.5G Samsung SSD 860 /dev/sdb 57.3G SanDisk Ultra Disk to ERASE and install onto (e.g. /dev/sda): The list is real disks only. Loop, ram and nbd devices are excluded by major number and zram by name — on a live medium the squashfs alone adds a loop device, and an early version of this screen showed twenty entries on a machine with one SSD. The installer refuses the medium you booted from, so picking the USB stick out of the list cannot destroy the installer mid-run. It also refuses a disk with mounted partitions, or one held open by LVM, RAID or an existing LUKS co"},{"t":"2. Hostname","p":"Install from the ATI-OS USB","u":"install-usb.html#q-host","b":"Hostname [ati-os]:"},{"t":"3. Username","p":"Install from the ATI-OS USB","u":"install-usb.html#q-user","b":"Username: Lowercase letters, digits, - and _ , not starting with a digit. It re-asks until the name is valid. The username is not cosmetic. arch-config keys the host profile off the username . The YAML field is called host: but it holds a username — a quirk of dcli's config schema, documented at length in arch-config.sh . So this answer decides which package set and dotfile profile get applied. It also becomes the name rendered on the boot splash."},{"t":"4. Password","p":"Install from the ATI-OS USB","u":"install-usb.html#q-pass","b":"Password for <user>: Repeat: Not echoed. Re-asks if empty or mismatched. The same password is set for root ."},{"t":"5. Timezone","p":"Install from the ATI-OS USB","u":"install-usb.html#q-tz","b":"Timezone [Europe/Istanbul]: The default is guessed from your IP via ipapi.co , with a 5-second timeout, falling back to UTC . An unknown zone aborts rather than silently installing the wrong one."},{"t":"6. Full-disk encryption","p":"Install from the ATI-OS USB","u":"install-usb.html#q-enc","b":"Encrypt the whole disk? Yes: a stolen machine reveals nothing. You type a passphrase at every boot, and if you forget it the data is GONE for good. Recommended for a laptop. Not needed for a desktop at home. Encrypt? [y/N]: Defaults to no, deliberately. Saying yes asks for a passphrase twice and requires at least 8 characters — LUKS is only as strong as what is typed into it, and a brute-forceable phrase is worse than no encryption because it feels safe. There is no recovery mechanism, by design."},{"t":"7. Keyboard layout","p":"Install from the ATI-OS USB","u":"install-usb.html#q-kb","b":"Keyboard layout (see them all with: localectl list-keymaps) Keymap [us]: Validated against localectl list-keymaps and re-asked until it matches. This is asked rather than assumed because the first version of the installer wrote no /etc/vconsole.conf at all: every machine came up with a US console keymap whatever hardware it had, so somebody on an AZERTY or Arabic keyboard would type their own password wrong at the very first login with no way to tell why."},{"t":"8. System language","p":"Install from the ATI-OS USB","u":"install-usb.html#q-locale","b":"System language (e.g. en_US.UTF-8, ar_EG.UTF-8, de_DE.UTF-8) Locale [en_US.UTF-8]: Validated against /etc/locale.gen . Whatever you pick, en_US.UTF-8 is also generated, because a surprising amount of tooling assumes it exists and misbehaves confusingly when it does not."},{"t":"What happens at each stage","p":"Install from the ATI-OS USB","u":"install-usb.html#stages","b":"After the last question the installer runs without further input. Each stage announces itself with a ── name ── header. Stage What it does partitioning Writes the partition table. If encryption was chosen, cryptsetup luksFormat --type luks2 then opens it as cryptroot . From here on, a failure leaves a half-installed disk — the installer says so plainly, because the recovery is always the same: run it again. mirrors Mirrors are ranked, not asked about. package signing keys Refreshes archlinux-keyring from the network first, so the new system's keyring is not limited to what shipped on the stick"},{"t":"The first boot","p":"Install from the ATI-OS USB","u":"install-usb.html#firstboot","b":"The desktop is not installed during the install. It is installed on the first boot, by ati-os-firstboot.service , which runs the same wizard.sh the git-clone route uses — all 46 modules, on screen, roughly 20 minutes. Let it finish before logging in. cat /var/log/ati-os-firstboot.log # the whole run cat /var/lib/ati-os/firstboot-complete # the wizard's exit status The marker records the exit status , not merely that the run happened, so a failed first boot does not mark itself done and will retry. To re-run by hand: cd ~/.dotfiles/installScripts && ./wizard.sh Read the per-module errors, not t"},{"t":"Logging in","p":"Install from the ATI-OS USB","u":"install-usb.html#login","b":"There is no autologin and no display manager . The wizard's disable-dm module disables lightdm , gdm , sddm and lxdm on purpose. After the first boot finishes you are looking at a text console with a blinking cursor. Log in with the username and password you chose. Type letsgo , or startx if that is not found. letsgo is a fish function , not a shell alias, so it exists only once the account's login shell is fish. The wizard's login-shell module does that with chsh , but only when fish installed successfully — if it did not, startx is the one that works."},{"t":"What you still supply","p":"Install from the ATI-OS USB","u":"install-usb.html#after","b":"GEMINI_API_KEY in ~/.config/secrets.env — the install seeds that file from a template with the key left empty. Everything that uses it degrades rather than breaking when it is absent. Vaultwarden, Simplenote and scrcpy accounts — see Everyday tools . Your own wallpapers, if you do not want the author's collection. Deliberately not installed by default: ./wizard.sh --only=xmodmap # Caps-Lock-as-Alt ./wizard.sh --only=dcli-sync-extra # docker, JDK, printing, qemu"},{"t":"Limits","p":"Install from the ATI-OS USB","u":"install-usb.html#limits","b":"UEFI only. The installed system boots with systemd-boot, and every boot module in this repo ( boot-fallback , boot-splash , and boot-splash 's root-device verification) is written against it. A GRUB/BIOS path would be a second, untested way to boot. It does not resize partitions. Alongside-install uses free space that is already free. The qemu test cannot touch the GPU path — no graphics hardware, so picom, glx and the window animations are untested by every phase of it. No AMD or NVIDIA hardware, and no 4K panel , has ever run this. The test boots the ISO file , not a real USB stick on real f"},{"t":"Install from a git clone","p":"Install from a git clone","u":"install-git.html","b":"You already have Arch installed — a clean base system, no desktop environment, no display manager. One script builds the rest."},{"t":"The one command","p":"Install from a git clone","u":"install-git.html#one","b":"git clone https://github.com/Mohamedattiadev/Newdotfile-.git ~/.dotfiles \\ && cd ~/.dotfiles/installScripts \\ && ./install.sh 46 modules run end to end with no questions asked, and you land on a complete desktop: qtile, all 22 themes, every font, every widget, and a boot splash carrying your own username. Set aside an hour or two, and about 25 GB. Nothing is wrong if it seems to sit still — most of that time is one package. pacman-static compiles pacman and every dependency statically from source, and several AUR packages (espanso, whisper.cpp) are built rather than downloaded. It is unattende"},{"t":"Then log in and start X","p":"Install from a git clone","u":"install-git.html#login","b":"There is no autologin and no display manager. Module 38, disable-dm , disables lightdm , gdm , sddm and lxdm on purpose. Nothing starts X for you. Log in at the TTY with your username and password. Type letsgo , or startx if that is not found. letsgo is a fish function , not a shell alias, so it exists only when the account's login shell is fish. Module 20, login-shell , runs chsh to fish so the TTY matches what kitty already forces — but it skips itself with a message if fish is not installed yet. On such a machine only startx exists. What letsgo adds over bare startx : it refuses when an X s"},{"t":"Why it never asks anything","p":"Install from a git clone","u":"install-git.html#noask","b":"install.sh is wizard.sh --yes . Every module runs with stdin closed and the wizard refuses to reach any prompt in that mode. This is a guarantee, not a description — it used to be untrue in two places: the run stopped dead at the very end on a Simplenote password prompt, and any module whose child process asked a question (a git credential, a makepkg confirmation) hung forever behind the spinner , showing nothing but an animation. Both are fixed. What ./install.sh does on top of running the modules: Auto-bootstraps gum via pacman (~2 s) Keeps sudo alive for the whole run — primed once, refresh"},{"t":"The 46 modules","p":"Install from a git clone","u":"install-git.html#modules","b":"Numbered in the order they actually run. This list is generated from wizard.sh 's own MOD_ORDER rather than maintained by hand. # id What 1 sanity Sanity checks (Arch, X11, dotfiles present) 2 bootstrap Bootstrap packages (git, stow, xorg-server, base-devel) 3 yay Build yay-bin from the AUR if absent 4 dcli Install dcli-arch-git 5 stow Stow dotfiles into $HOME — backs up anything already there to ~/DefaultConfig/<timestamp>/ 6 arch-config Sync the arch-config host file to the current username 7 paths Expand @HOME@ in browser flags, gtk.css and bookmarks for this user 8 dcli-sync dcli sync --fo"},{"t":"The two opt-in modules","p":"Install from a git clone","u":"install-git.html#optin","b":"Both are valid --only= targets and both are listed (unchecked) in the interactive picker, but neither is ever part of a default run. id What, and why it is opt-in xmodmap Writes ~/.Xmodmap , repurposing Caps Lock as Alt_L outright , with no tap-to-Caps-Lock fallback. Right for one laptop whose Alt key is dead in hardware, merely surprising everywhere else. Real Alt keys keep working, so on a normal machine this just gives you a third Alt. ./wizard.sh --yes --only=xmodmap dcli-sync-extra docker, JDK, printing, qemu — see Optional extras . install.sh 's promise is a working desktop , not a worki"},{"t":"Install options","p":"Install from a git clone","u":"install-git.html#options","b":"./wizard.sh # interactive: TUI checkbox picker ./wizard.sh --dry-run # preview every command, touch nothing ./wizard.sh --yes # same as ./install.sh ./wizard.sh --only=stow,themes,browser-flags # subset ./wizard.sh --skip=whisper,whisper-fast,piper # skip heavy downloads ./wizard.sh --yes --only=dcli-sync-extra # opt-in extras ./wizard.sh --audit # package drift check (read-only, no sudo) ./wizard.sh --uninstall # reverse wizard writes (never touches # packages or downloaded models) ./wizard.sh --uninstall --dry-run # preview reversals --only and --skip need the = . --only foo is rejected , no"},{"t":"The three accounts","p":"Install from a git clone","u":"install-git.html#accounts","b":"There are no manual follow-up commands to get a working desktop. What is left are three accounts, and only you can own an account. Do them whenever you like, in any order, days later is fine. What it unlocks Without it Simplenote The Super + Shift + S TODOS note syncs to the phone app The note still works — it is a local file. Only the phone mirror is missing Vaultwarden Super + p p password picker No password menu Gemini (optional) AI synonyms and example sentences in the translators and rofi_anki All three tools still work; the AI section is omitted, not broken"},{"t":"Simplenote","p":"Install from a git clone","u":"install-git.html#simplenote","b":"Run it without --yes , so it can ask, and run it after your first desktop login — install.sh finishes in a TTY before startx , and this step may need a browser: cd ~/.dotfiles/installScripts && ./wizard.sh --only=simplenote It asks for your Simplenote email and password, then proves it worked by pushing the TODOS note for real. On networks that block auth.simperium.com — the login host, which is separate from the reachable note API — it detects that, copies a one-line snippet to your clipboard for the browser console, and takes an access token instead. That is the part that needs a browser. On"},{"t":"Vaultwarden","p":"Install from a git clone","u":"install-git.html#vaultwarden","b":"The installer already started the server on 127.0.0.1:8222 and pointed rbw at it. The account is yours to create: Open https://127.0.0.1:8222 and register. The s matters — the web vault refuses plain http. The local certificate is already trusted by the browser, because mkcert installs its CA into the NSS store too. Press Super + p then p and enter that email when asked. rbw prompts for the master password through pinentry and keeps the vault unlocked for 15 minutes. Details, including the phone app and the Tailscale route: Everyday tools → Passwords ."},{"t":"Gemini","p":"Install from a git clone","u":"install-git.html#gemini","b":"Put GEMINI_API_KEY=… in ~/.config/secrets.env . The install seeds that file from secrets.env.example with the key left empty, so it is already there with instructions in it. chmod 600 , and it is gitignored."},{"t":"Optional extras","p":"Install from a git clone","u":"install-git.html#extras","b":"The default install deliberately leaves out packages that have nothing to do with the desktop. Skipping them changes nothing about qtile, the themes, the fonts or the widgets. Run this whenever you actually want them: ~/.dotfiles/installScripts/wizard.sh --yes --only=dcli-sync-extra Containers docker · docker-buildx · docker-compose Dev extras github-cli · git-lfs · clang · jdk17-openjdk · uv · ruff Virtualisation qemu-desktop · edk2-ovmf — only vm-test.sh needs these Printing cups · cups-pk-helper · system-config-printer Diagnostics xorg-server-xephyr (test a qtile config in a nested X) · mes"},{"t":"Post-install tuning","p":"Install from a git clone","u":"install-git.html#tuning","b":"Two interactive scripts, not wired into install.sh because they need a reboot, are per-machine, and prompt before touching anything. Both are idempotent, back up before writing, and print revert instructions at the end. bash ~/.dotfiles/installScripts/grub_boost.sh # kernel cmdline: nowatchdog, # quiet loglevel=3, cursor off, i915 GuC bash ~/.dotfiles/installScripts/service_trim.sh # audit + disable heavy services grub_boost.sh refuses rather than guessing . It aborts on a trailing-comment or unquoted GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT instead of corrupting it, and prints the offending line. A small n"},{"t":"Trying it without risking a machine","p":"Install from a git clone","u":"install-git.html#testing","b":"Four layers, cheapest first. Each catches what the one above cannot. Layer Time Catches validate.sh seconds Syntax, qtile config load, hardcoded home paths, unrendered templates wizard.sh --audit seconds Declared packages vs installed, both directions container-test.sh ~3 min A real install as a user who is not you; idempotency vm-test.sh --unattended ~2 hours X11, systemd, boot, the desktop — the parts nothing else can reach bash ~/.dotfiles/installScripts/validate.sh bash ~/.dotfiles/installScripts/container-test.sh --check # verify runtime bash ~/.dotfiles/installScripts/container-test.sh #"},{"t":"Keybindings","p":"Keybindings","u":"keybindings.html","b":"Everything below was read out of .config/qtile/config.py . If a binding is not here, it is not bound."},{"t":"The two modifiers","p":"Keybindings","u":"keybindings.html#modifiers","b":"Name here In the config X modifier Super mod mod4 — the Windows key Alt mod2 mod1 — the Alt key Caps Lock is not Alt unless you asked for it. The xmodmap module that repurposes Caps Lock as Alt_L is opt-in and not part of a default install — it has no tap-to-Caps fallback, which is right for one laptop whose Alt key is dead in hardware and merely surprising everywhere else. On a default install, use your real Alt key. To opt in: ./wizard.sh --yes --only=xmodmap ."},{"t":"Windows and layouts","p":"Keybindings","u":"keybindings.html#windows","b":"Keys Action Super + h Move focus left. On max and treetab , which have no \"left\", this falls back to previous Super + l Move focus right (or next on max / treetab ) Super + j Move focus down Super + k Move focus up Super + Shift + h Move the window left; in treetab , move the tab left Super + Shift + l Move the window right; in treetab , move the tab right Super + Shift + j Move the window down; in treetab , move down a section Super + Shift + Space Toggle between split and unsplit sides of the stack Super + x Toggle the focused window between minimum and maximum size Super + t Toggle floating"},{"t":"Workspaces","p":"Keybindings","u":"keybindings.html#groups","b":"Nine groups plus a scratchpad. Group S (Anki, Obsidian, the nvimsum window) has no key of its own by design — you reach it via the application bindings. Keys Action Super + 1 … 9 Switch to that group Super + Shift + 1 … 9 Move the focused window to that group Inside most modes the plain digits 1 … 9 also switch groups, so you never get stuck in a chord on the wrong workspace. That applies to rofi, media, resize, draw, hint and cheatsheet mode. Group Default layout Applications that land there 1 MonadTall ticktick 2 Max qutebrowser, zen-browser, vlc, ops, firefox 3 MonadTall Nautilus, pcmanfm-q"},{"t":"Launching and sessions","p":"Keybindings","u":"keybindings.html#launch","b":"The \"toggle\" bindings are session toggles, not plain launchers: they raise and focus the application if it is running, and start it if it is not. Keys Action Super + Enter Terminal ( kitty ) Super + Shift + Enter Run launcher ( rofi -show drun -show-icons ) Super + n Toggle the terminal session Super + b Toggle the Brave session Super + v Toggle the qutebrowser session Super + m Toggle the file manager session Super + Shift + t Toggle the Telegram session Super + Shift + o Open Obsidian to draw (assumes Excalidraw is installed in Obsidian) Super + Shift + s Open or focus sum.md globally, in ki"},{"t":"Session and machine","p":"Keybindings","u":"keybindings.html#session","b":"Keys Action Super + Shift + r Restart qtile. Goes through _smooth_restart , which raises the restart veil and preserves window→group and layout state Super + Shift + q Logout menu ( dm-logout -r ) Super + Shift + F5 Refresh the machine without rebooting ( reset_PC ) Super + Shift + F6 Mirror the Android phone ( phone_screen , USB or Wi-Fi) Super + Shift + z Toggle between the top and bottom bar Super + ` Toggle the second system widget box Print Screenshot an area straight to the clipboard — the same action as the bar's camera chip F8 Start/stop live voice dictation — types each phrase the ins"},{"t":"Homerow (mouse-free clicking)","p":"Keybindings","u":"keybindings.html#homerow","b":"Homerow -style hinting over AT-SPI, so it targets real UI elements with exact bounds rather than guessing at pixels. Each mode has its own Alt binding as well as a letter inside hint mode , because hinting is something you do constantly and a chord costs a keystroke plus remembering you are in a mode. Keys Action Alt + Space Hint and click / switch window Alt + j Scroll mode Alt + / Search mode Alt + c Caret mode — vim motions over real text Alt + Shift + c Caret search — type to find a word, land the caret there Alt + e Edit a field in nvim, in place Alt + Space rather than Homerow's own Shif"},{"t":"Other Alt bindings","p":"Keybindings","u":"keybindings.html#alt","b":"Keys Action Alt + Shift + d Toggle qdrop , the drop-stash Alt + v CopyQ clipboard picker in rofi — Ctrl + j / k to navigate, with thumbnails Alt + p Clock popup: today and this week's plans and todos Alt + n Dismiss the top notification ( dunstctl close ) Alt + Tab Toggle the systray widget box Alt + ` Toggle the system widget box"},{"t":"Scratchpads","p":"Keybindings","u":"keybindings.html#scratch","b":"Dropdown windows that appear over whatever group you are on and hide again on the same key. Keys Window Alt + 1 term1 — kitty Alt + 2 term2 — kitty Alt + 5 calc — qalculate-gtk Alt + 8 WhatsApp Alt + 9 DeepSeek Alt + 0 ChatGPT Alt + 3 and Alt + 4 used to be the pavucontrol and arandr scratchpads. They keep their meaning — audio and displays — but open the audio and display popups instead of a GTK window. The three web scratchpads are Brave profiles inside the main Brave instance ( --profile-directory ), not separate browsers. A separate --user-data-dir cannot share anything with the main insta"},{"t":"Mouse","p":"Keybindings","u":"keybindings.html#mouse","b":"Input Action Super + drag left button Move a floating window Super + drag right button Resize a floating window Super + middle click Bring the window to the front follow_mouse_focus is on; bring_front_click and cursor_warp are off."},{"t":"Modes and chords","p":"Keybindings","u":"keybindings.html#modes","b":"A mode is a qtile KeyChord that takes over the keyboard and announces itself as a chip in the bar, listing the keys it accepts. Esc leaves — qtile appends its own Escape binding to every chord, so it always works even where the tables below do not list it. Entry Mode Sticky? Super + P Rofi mode No — runs one action and leaves Super + P then w Wallpaper picker Yes Super + P then n WiFi Yes Super + P then b Bluetooth Yes Alt + 3 Audio Yes — the same key closes it Alt + 4 Display Yes — the same key closes it Super + / Media Yes Super + r Resize Yes Super + Shift + w Draw Yes Super + Shift + f Hin"},{"t":"Super+P — rofi mode","p":"Keybindings","u":"keybindings.html#rofi-mode","b":"The launcher hub. It is not sticky: one key runs one thing and the mode ends. Three of its letters open sticky sub-modes instead. Key Action Command c Theme picker theme-toggle w Wallpaper picker (sub-mode) — n WiFi (sub-mode) — b Bluetooth (sub-mode) — p Passwords, over Vaultwarden rofi_pass l Screen brightness rofi_light t Todo manager rofi_todo k Kill processes rofi-kill a Add an Anki note rofi_anki e Translate text rofi_translator/wordreference.py s Spell check menu dm-spellcheck -r i Screenshot with annotation dm-satty r Record video / audio / webcam / gif dm-recordV2 d Show documents dm-"},{"t":"Wallpaper picker — Super+P then w","p":"Keybindings","u":"keybindings.html#wallpaper-mode","b":"Key Action h / l Move left / right j / k Move down / up r Jump to a random wallpaper / Fuzzy search in rofi Enter Apply q / Esc Close Changing the wallpaper only re-derives the colour palette when the active theme mode is already wal . On a preset you chose a fixed palette on purpose, so a new wallpaper swaps the desktop image and nothing else. See The themes ."},{"t":"WiFi — Super+P then n","p":"Keybindings","u":"keybindings.html#wifi-mode","b":"A keyboard-driven picker over nmcli . It never opens nm-connection-editor ; the applet stays in the tray as an icon only. Key Action j / k Move down / up g / Shift + g Jump to top / bottom Enter Connect d Disconnect x Forget the network n Connect to a hidden network t Toggle the radio s Share the selected saved network to a phone as a QR code r Rescan / Search c Cancel a connect that is hanging q / Esc Close A wrong password re-asks instead of leaving a saved profile that can never connect."},{"t":"Bluetooth — Super+P then b","p":"Keybindings","u":"keybindings.html#bt-mode","b":"Everything happens in the popup — scan, pair, trust, connect, disconnect, remove. blueman's window is not involved. Key Action j / k Move down / up g / Shift + g Jump to top / bottom Enter Connect d Disconnect x Remove the device t Toggle the radio r Scan / Search c Abort a pair or connect that is hanging — bluetoothctl connect never returns for an out-of-range device q / Esc Close"},{"t":"Audio — Alt+3","p":"Keybindings","u":"keybindings.html#audio-mode","b":"Covers what pavucontrol did. Pressing Alt + 3 again closes it. Key Action j / k Move down / up g / Shift + g Jump to top / bottom Tab / Shift + Tab Cycle view forwards / backwards o Outputs view i Inputs view a Playback (per-application) view s Recording view p Card profiles Shift + p Ports — the thing that picks Headphones over Speakers on one card Shift + c Every card at once (pavucontrol's Configuration tab) Enter Activate. On an output this also drags the running streams over — setting the default alone only affects what starts playing afterwards d Send the selected stream to the default d"},{"t":"Display — Alt+4","p":"Keybindings","u":"keybindings.html#display-mode","b":"Covers what arandr did, plus saved layouts, which arandr did not. Pressing Alt + 4 again closes it. Key Action j / k Move the cursor. In arrange mode these move the picked monitor instead g / Shift + g Jump to top / bottom Tab Pick the next output a Start arranging Enter Open the resolution / refresh list for the selected output, and apply from inside it Backspace Back i Internal display only e External display only m Mirror h / l Place left / right u / d Place above / below = Cycle cross-axis alignment while arranging: tops, centres, bottoms p Set primary t Rotate f Reflect o Toggle the outpu"},{"t":"Media — Super+/","p":"Keybindings","u":"keybindings.html#media-mode","b":"Key Action Shift + k / Shift + j Volume up / down by 5 Shift + m Mute Shift + l / Shift + h Brightness up / down by 5 Shift + p Toggle mpv picture-in-picture mode 1 … 9 Switch group q / Esc Leave"},{"t":"Resize — Super+r","p":"Keybindings","u":"keybindings.html#resize-mode","b":"Key Action Shift + h Shrink (MonadTall / MonadWide) Shift + l Grow (MonadTall / MonadWide) Shift + n Reset the layout's sizes 1 … 9 Switch group q / Esc Leave"},{"t":"Draw — Super+Shift+w","p":"Keybindings","u":"keybindings.html#draw-mode","b":"A gromit-mpx overlay for drawing on the screen. Key Action w Toggle drawing ( gromit-mpx -t ) c Clear z Undo r Redo v Toggle visibility 1 … 9 Switch group q / Esc Leave"},{"t":"Hint — Super+Shift+f","p":"Keybindings","u":"keybindings.html#hint-mode","b":"The same Homerow actions as the Alt bindings above, discoverable in one place. Each action leaves the mode. It used to persist so actions could be chained, but this chord swallows every other binding on the desktop while it is open — including the ones that reload qtile — and a chord you cannot tell you are inside is indistinguishable from the keyboard having broken. Key Action h Hint and click elements / switch window s Pick a scrollable region, drive it with vim keys f Search elements — digits pick, Enter clicks v Caret mode: vim motions over real text, v selects, y yanks Shift + v Caret sea"},{"t":"Language switch — Super+Space","p":"Keybindings","u":"keybindings.html#lang-mode","b":"Key Layout e us — English a ara — Arabic t tr — Turkish d de — German q / Esc Leave These four are bound by X keycode (26, 38, 28, 40) rather than by keysym, so they stay on the same physical keys whatever layout is currently active — which is the point, since you press them precisely when the layout is wrong. The letters shown are the US-layout positions."},{"t":"Cheatsheets — Super+Shift+k","p":"Keybindings","u":"keybindings.html#cheat-mode","b":"Key Action k Open the qtile sheet when none is up; scroll up when one is j Scroll down a few rows v Show the vim cheatsheet f Show the fish + kitty cheatsheet Tab Next screenful Shift + Tab Previous screenful 1 … 9 Switch group q / Esc Exit cheatsheet mode Only one sheet is ever open at a time — v and f close whichever is showing before opening theirs, which is what makes \"the open one\" unambiguous for j , k and Tab . Modifier names render as symbols ( ⇧ Shift, ⌃ Ctrl, ⏎ Enter, ␣ Space) with the legend in the header."},{"t":"Passthrough — Super+F12","p":"Keybindings","u":"keybindings.html#passthrough","b":"Releases qtile's grabs so an application can receive keys qtile would otherwise take — a VM, a remote desktop, a nested WM. Super + F12 again, or Esc , ends it. Unlike the other modes it does not swallow, so everything else still reaches the application."},{"t":"Getting this list live","p":"Keybindings","u":"keybindings.html#live","b":"The desktop generates the same list from the config it is actually running, so it cannot drift: qtile-keys # every binding with a description, on stdout rofi_docs keys # the searchable rofi list rofi_docs # the whole documentation menu Or click the logo in the bar and pick Keybindings . That list is built by parsing config.py 's AST rather than with a regex, because bindings nest several KeyChord levels deep — a regex pass found 44 of them and lost every chord prefix, so mode keys printed as a bare letter instead of Super+P , C . qtile-keys lists only bindings that carry a desc= . Several real"},{"t":"The themes","p":"The themes","u":"themes.html","b":"Pick a theme and everything follows — bar, terminal, rofi, notifications, GTK, Qt, browser chrome, nvim, the boot splash, even the folder icons. 10 of the 22 modes."},{"t":"Switching","p":"The themes","u":"themes.html#switch","b":"theme-apply gruvbox # any preset theme-apply wal # follow the current wallpaper theme-toggle # the rofi picker Or press Super + P then c . The picker shows friendly names ( wal appears as Wallpaper ) and marks the current theme with ● . Rapid picker clicks preempt: an in-flight theme-apply is killed and the newer one starts, rather than the second click being silently dropped."},{"t":"The 22 modes","p":"The themes","u":"themes.html#list","b":"21 fixed presets plus wal , which derives its palette from the current wallpaper. Mode Mode Mode doomone (default) kanagawa cyberpunk-neon dracula oxocarbon synthwave gruvbox onedark matrix nord palenight mono-dark tokyonight nightowl mono-light catppuccin github-dark wal — from the wallpaper monokai ayu-mirage everforest rose-pine Each preset is nine hex colours in theme-apply : background, alternate background, foreground, then red, green, yellow, blue, purple and cyan."},{"t":"Light mode","p":"The themes","u":"themes.html#light","b":"mono-light is the one light preset. It flips the base GTK theme to Breeze + Papirus-Light ; every other mode uses Sweet-Dark + Papirus-Dark . That is what makes GTK apps render properly light-on-white rather than dark widgets on a light palette. The file manager ( pcmanfm-qt ) is a Qt application, so it ignores the GTK theme entirely and follows the generated qt6ct / qt5ct palette instead — same colours, different path."},{"t":"What retints, and how","p":"The themes","u":"themes.html#retints","b":"Fifteen separate consumers, each with its own reload mechanism, because none of them share one. Consumer Reload mechanism kitty set-colors --all per live socket, plus SIGUSR1 rofi A current-palette.rasi symlink swap dunst Render dunstrc.tmpl , then restart qtile restart , detached so the caller does not deadlock GTK 3 / 4 An @import overlay at ~/.cache/qtile/gtk-wal.css Qt 5 / Qt 6 (Telegram, pcmanfm-qt, …) A 21-role QPalette written to ~/.config/qt6ct/colors/current.conf and the qt5ct equivalent. Needs QT_QPA_PLATFORMTHEME=qt6ct , exported from .xinitrc — Qt ignores the GTK theme entirely and"},{"t":"Wallpaper mode","p":"The themes","u":"themes.html#wal","b":"theme-apply wal derives the palette from the current wallpaper, using a precompiled cache. wal-precompile walks ~/Pictures/Wallpapers/ and produces a per-image palette at ~/.cache/qtile/palettes/<basename>.json , each one forced to a doomone-quality bar: WCAG AAA background/foreground contrast, WCAG AA per accent, and a guaranteed hue spread. Drop any image into ~/Pictures/Wallpapers/ and select it — theme-apply wal auto-runs wal-precompile --only <basename> on a cache miss. There is no manual step."},{"t":"Palette semantics","p":"The themes","u":"themes.html#palette","b":"Six accent slots, hue-concentrated rather than evenly spread: Slot Role color1 Urgent — always warm color2 Dominant — the main wallpaper hue. Bar accents pin here color3 Warm fill color4 Cool fill color5 Complement color6 Info / cyan A test harness at .config/qtile/scripts/wal-visual-test.py validates 12 hue buckets end to end."},{"t":"Wallpaper vs. theme","p":"The themes","u":"themes.html#wallpaper-vs-theme","b":"Changing the wallpaper re-derives the palette only when the active mode is already wal . On a preset you picked a fixed palette on purpose, so a new wallpaper swaps the desktop image and nothing else. All three wallpaper setters ( dm-setbg , the dmscripts dm-setbg , and the qtile WallpaperPopup ) check ~/.cache/qtile/theme_mode before invoking theme-apply wal on a background thread, and fail closed if that file is unreadable."},{"t":"The boot splash","p":"The themes","u":"themes.html#splash","b":"The splash follows your theme too, but it is the one consumer that cannot update instantly: plymouth runs before / is mounted, so the theme is embedded in the initramfs . Rebuilding that is about a minute — far too slow for a colour change. So theme-apply regenerates the assets and then tells you the boot screen is out of date. You do the rebuild when you want it: boot-splash status # what is installed, hooked and set boot-splash sync # rebuild the initramfs to match the current theme The full command set: boot-splash generate # render + install the theme (touches nothing about boot) boot-spla"},{"t":"What it draws","p":"The themes","u":"themes.html#splash-what","b":"The Arch mark inside a progress ring, with your username underneath as ANSI Shadow block art, in the colours of the active theme. The name is generated, never hardcoded: user ati gets ATI , user beko gets BEKO . Override it with BOOT_SPLASH_NAME : BOOT_SPLASH_NAME=\"Mohamed Attia\" boot-splash generate && boot-splash preview BOOT_SPLASH_SIZE=32 boot-splash generate # bigger, if you want it Two things move, so a slow boot never looks like a hung one: a comet sweeps continuously around the ring — the liveness signal, which keeps moving even when plymouth reports no progress at all, which is most o"},{"t":"One continuous image","p":"The themes","u":"themes.html#splash-uki","b":"The splash used to be bookended by Arch's own logo, for ~3s before and ~4s after. Neither was plymouth's doing: Arch's linux.preset ships --splash /usr/share/systemd/bootctl/splash-arch.bmp , which bakes that bitmap into the UKI as a PE .splash section. systemd-boot paints it the moment a boot entry is picked, plymouth draws over it, and when plymouth quits its buffer is released and the bitmap shows through again. So generate also renders a static BMP of the same composition at the firmware's framebuffer resolution, and enable / sync point every UKI-building preset at it instead. The presets "},{"t":"Three safety properties","p":"The themes","u":"themes.html#splash-safety","b":"The LTS rescue entries stay verbose. boot-fallback strips quiet and splash from their options. A rescue entry that inherited the splash would show a logo while hiding the kernel messages saying what broke — indistinguishable from the failed boot you are escaping. enable refuses unless check passes. Sixteen checks run first: plymouth installed, colour placeholders substituted, theme script braces balanced, plymouth recognises the theme, the static boot frame is uncompressed 24-bit BMP3 (systemd-boot reads nothing else and silently draws blank for anything else), the UKI presets point at it, a v"},{"t":"State files","p":"The themes","u":"themes.html#state","b":"All auto-created; there is no manual bootstrap. Path Contents ~/.cache/qtile/theme_mode The active mode name ~/.cache/qtile/current_palette.json The 9-slot palette, dumped on every apply. Consumed by nvim, the popups and eww ~/.cache/qtile/palettes/<name>.json Precompiled per-wallpaper palettes ~/.cache/qtile/layout_state.json MonadTall ratios and relative sizes per group ~/.cache/qtile/window_group_state.json window id → group map, plus per-group focus order ~/.cache/wall Symlink to the active wallpaper"},{"t":"Concurrency","p":"The themes","u":"themes.html#concurrency","b":"theme-apply holds a flock on ~/.cache/qtile/.theme-apply.lock . Keybind spam is dropped rather than corrupting caches; picker clicks preempt instead of being dropped."},{"t":"What is deliberately not themed","p":"The themes","u":"themes.html#notweb","b":"Web page content. The dark-mode module sets org.gnome.desktop.interface color-scheme=prefer-dark , which xdg-desktop-portal republishes as org.freedesktop.appearance color-scheme = 1 . Chromium reads that key and reports prefers-color-scheme: dark , so each site serves its own dark stylesheet. The browser chrome stays palette-driven. --enable-features=WebContentsForceDark used to sit in all three *-flags.conf . It is a per-pixel colour transform applied after render, with no knowledge of the page's design — fine on a site with no dark theme, destructive on one that has a good one, because it r"},{"t":"Widgets and popups","p":"Widgets and popups","u":"widgets.html","b":"The bar, what each chip does when you click it, the keyboard-driven popups that replaced pavucontrol and arandr, and the veil that hides a qtile restart."},{"t":"The bar","p":"Widgets and popups","u":"widgets.html#bar","b":"Two bars are defined and one is shown at a time. Super + Shift + z swaps between the top bar and the bottom bar — only one is ever exclusive. Everything in the bar is built from a small set of qtile and qtile_extras widgets: GroupBox , TaskList , Chord , Clock , CPU , Memory , Battery , Systray , LaunchBar , TextBox and Spacer , wrapped in a local chip() helper that gives each one the rounded pill decoration."},{"t":"The chips, and what clicking does","p":"Widgets and popups","u":"widgets.html#chips","b":"Chip Click The logo Left — the documentation menu . Middle — a terminal. Right — rofi -show drun Camera Screenshot an area straight to the clipboard — the same action as the bare Print key CPU / memory Opens btop in a fullscreen terminal Updates Toggles qupdate Disk disk_notify — a dunst popup with usage for / and /home Battery battery_notify — charge level and state. The chip is only added to the bar at all when a battery exists, so a desktop does not get one Keyboard layout Cycles to the next layout Clock clock_popup — today and this week's plans and todos. Also on Alt + p Media Left — playe"},{"t":"Widget boxes","p":"Widgets and popups","u":"widgets.html#boxes","b":"Three collapsible groups, so the bar can carry more than fits. Box Key system_widgetbox Alt + ` 2nd_system_widgetbox Super + ` systray_widgetbox (the △ chip) Alt + Tab Entering any chord closes an open widget box and remembers it, so a mode chip is never competing for space with an expanded box."},{"t":"The logo menu","p":"Widgets and popups","u":"widgets.html#logo","b":"Left-clicking the logo opens rofi_docs . A desktop with this many keybindings, themes and custom tools has a discovery problem, not a terminal-launching problem — so the most prominent click in the bar answers \"what can this thing do\" . Seven sections, and every one is generated from the live system , because a hand-written menu drifts the moment anything is added, and silently. Section What it lists Where it comes from Keybindings Every shortcut, searchable config.py , parsed by qtile-keys via Python's AST Cheatsheets qtile · vim · fish popups qtile IPC, not replayed keystrokes Documentation "},{"t":"Espanso snippets","p":"Widgets and popups","u":"widgets.html#espanso","b":"The snippets in match/base.yml hold no personal data — they shell out to source /etc/environment; echo \"$MY_NAME\" . That is the right design and it has a sharp edge: on a machine where those variables are unset, every one of them expands to an empty string , with no error. The Espanso section of the logo menu lists which of the 26 variables are set and which are missing, scaffolds the missing keys into /etc/environment (commented, ready to fill), and opens it with sudoedit ."},{"t":"The qtile popups","p":"Widgets and popups","u":"widgets.html#popups","b":"These are qtile_extras popups drawn by qtile itself, not separate applications. They live in .config/qtile/popups/ . Popup Opened by What it replaces AudioPopup Alt + 3 pavucontrol DisplayPopup Alt + 4 arandr , plus saved layouts, which arandr had no equivalent of WifiPopup Super + P then n nm-connection-editor WifiQR s inside the WiFi popup — BluetoothPopup Super + P then b the blueman window WallpaperPopup Super + P then w , or the ✖ chip — QtileCheatsheet Super + Shift + k then k — VimCheatsheet Super + Shift + k then v — FishCheatsheet Super + Shift + k then f — UpdatesPopup — not bound in"},{"t":"The cheatsheets","p":"Widgets and popups","u":"widgets.html#cheatsheets","b":"Every binding on a card, with the key hard against the card's right edge, in the same monospace face the rest of the popups use. The sheets scroll rather than page: j / k move a few rows, Tab / Shift + Tab a screenful, Esc closes, and the header shows how far down you are. Scrolling is also why the sheets are an ordinary popup size rather than nearly full-screen. Paging tied capacity to size — the only way to show more bindings was to be bigger, and they had grown to 1330×750 on a 1366×768 screen, covering the very window you opened them to ask about. With a viewport that moves, the size only "},{"t":"HiDPI scaling","p":"Widgets and popups","u":"widgets.html#scaling","b":"Every pixel value in the qtile config was tuned on a 1366×768 14\" panel. Copied unchanged to a 15\" 4K laptop that is a sliver of a bar with unreadable text — the one thing these dotfiles cannot keep identical by copying files, because the right answer depends on the glass. ui-scale computes a factor from the primary display's real DPI and writes it to ~/.cache/qtile/ui_scale (per-machine, untracked). qtile multiplies every font size, bar height, icon and margin by it; Xft.dpi carries the same factor into GTK, Qt, rofi and dunst. It runs from .xinitrc on every login, so docking to an external m"},{"t":"The eww widgets","p":"Widgets and popups","u":"widgets.html#eww","b":"eww carries the parts that are not qtile popups. Its configuration is in .config/eww/ . Window What it is onboarding-welcome First page of the tour onboarding-workspaces What the groups are onboarding-keybindings The keys worth knowing first onboarding-finish Last page bar-tooltip The tooltip the bar's tooltip chip opens csheet A cheatsheet window ( cheatsheet/cheatsheet.yuck ) The tour runs once , on the first graphical login after an install, via onboarding-first-run ; wizard.sh arms it by writing a stamp file at the end of a successful run. After that it is only reachable from the bar's too"},{"t":"The restart veil","p":"Widgets and popups","u":"widgets.html#veil","b":"Super + Shift + r — and any theme change — goes through _smooth_restart , which raises a veil over the transition: qtile/scripts/qtile-restart-veil.py , a separate process so it survives the execv . It frosts the desktop, shows a card carrying each window's real icon, and reports genuine progress from the incoming qtile — not a timer. It exists because qtile's own boot maps every window from every workspace for ~2 seconds before the startup hook fires, which is unfixable from config alone. The veil pauses dunst and keeps itself topmost by reacting to root-window restack events, so nothing land"},{"t":"Everyday tools","p":"Everyday tools","u":"tools.html","b":"About fifty commands live in .config/AtiScriptsV1/ , symlinked onto $PATH at /usr/local/bin by the ati-scripts module. Symlinked rather than copied, so an edit in the repo applies immediately."},{"t":"Everyday commands","p":"Everyday tools","u":"tools.html#everyday","b":"letsgo / startx Start the session from a TTY Super + Shift + r Restart qtile (keeps layout and window→group state) dcli sync Update the system, snapshotting first theme-apply <name> Switch theme Super + P then c Theme picker Alt + Shift + d Toggle qdrop fc-cache -fv Reload fonts Reload the qtile config without a full restart: qtile cmd-obj -o cmd -f reload_config"},{"t":"qdrop — drop things here, use them later","p":"Everyday tools","u":"tools.html#qdrop","b":"A lightweight GTK3 daemon that slides in from top-centre, stashes files, text, URLs and images, and lets you drag them back out into any application. It replaced the flatpak it.mijorus.collector . It is themed live from the active palette. Using it Alt + Shift + d toggles it. Shake the mouse while actually dragging a file, folder or image — any axis, two reversals within 1.2 seconds — and it comes to you. A click-drag carrying nothing (a text selection, a rubber band, panning) is ignored. Shaking while it is already open keeps it open instead of replaying the reveal. Drop a file, text, URL or "},{"t":"qupdate — updates and installs, no terminal","p":"Everyday tools","u":"tools.html#qupdate","b":"Click the updates chip in the bar. A floating GTK3 daemon with two tabs. Updates tab Lists pending pacman and AUR packages, from a parallel paru -Qu plus paru -Qua . Cache-first render from ~/.cache/qupdate.json , so it opens instantly; revalidation runs on a background thread. Per-package checkbox, a PKG / AUR badge, and oldver → newver . Refresh / All / None / filter. Footer: Update selected ( paru -S --needed ) or Full upgrade via the tool combo — which defaults to dcli sync , so timeshift snapshots and arch-config module state stay in sync. Run in background means no terminal: notify-send "},{"t":"theme-apply and theme-toggle","p":"Everyday tools","u":"tools.html#theming","b":"theme-apply gruvbox # any of the 21 presets theme-apply wal # follow the current wallpaper theme-toggle # the rofi picker; also Super+P then c The full list and everything a switch touches is on The themes ."},{"t":"The rofi menus","p":"Everyday tools","u":"tools.html#rofi","b":"All of these are on Super + P plus one key — see the rofi mode table for the bindings. What they actually do: Command What it does rofi_pass Password picker over Vaultwarden via rbw . See Passwords rofi_todo Todo manager rofi_light Screen brightness, via light rofi-kill Process killer, showing PID + process + window title in aligned columns (via wmctrl -lp and a PPID walk for browser subprocesses) rofi_anki Build an Anki note and send it over AnkiConnect on :8765 rofi_shared Shared-links picker with previews rofi_translator / wordreference.py Translate the selected text, with a dictionary tabl"},{"t":"Passwords","p":"Everyday tools","u":"tools.html#passwords","b":"Super + p then p opens a rofi picker over your vault. Enter copies the password and wipes the clipboard 30 seconds later. The hint line advertises only the three you reach for — ↵ copy, Alt + n new, Alt + x delete — but the full set is bound: Key Action ↵ Copy the password (clipboard cleared after 30s) Alt + u Copy the username Alt + t Copy the TOTP code Alt + o Open the entry's website Alt + a Type the password into the window that had focus — never touches the clipboard Alt + n New entry (generate a 24-character password, or type your own) Alt + e Edit — choose password or username Alt + x D"},{"t":"Android screen mirroring","p":"Everyday tools","u":"tools.html#phone","b":"Super + Shift + F6 puts the phone on screen, with mouse and keyboard control back to it. It works over USB or Wi-Fi; the script picks whichever is available and prefers the cable. The first time Turn on Wireless debugging on the phone once — Settings → Developer options → Wireless debugging. It survives reboots, so this is genuinely one time. Then press the key. A rofi window opens with a QR code and a text field. Either scan the QR with the phone (Wireless debugging → Pair device with QR code ), or tap Pair device with pairing code and type the 6 digits into the same window. Whichever finishe"},{"t":"Voice dictation","p":"Everyday tools","u":"tools.html#dictation","b":"Key Tool Model F8 voice_dictate_live — types each phrase the instant you pause Whisper base.en F9 voice_dictate — press to start, press again to stop and paste the transcript Whisper small.en Both run on whisper.cpp locally — nothing is sent anywhere. The batch path is whisper-cli plus sox plus xdotool ; the live path is whisper-stream in VAD sliding-window mode, with voice_dictate_live_typer.py reading its block output and typing only the new words of each block. The models are downloaded by the whisper module (~630 MB). The whisper-fast module then rebuilds whisper-cli and whisper-stream opt"},{"t":"Notes and todos","p":"Everyday tools","u":"tools.html#notes","b":"Trigger What Super + Shift + s Open or focus sum.md in kitty, globally Alt + p or the clock chip clock_popup — today and this week's plans and todos Super + P then t rofi_todo Super + P then o dm-note -r — store and copy notes The TODOS note syncs to the Simplenote phone app once you have configured the account ( ./wizard.sh --only=simplenote , without --yes ). Every write pushes and opening the window pulls. Editing offline on either side is safe — the pull doubles as the retry for an unsent write, and a push checks the note before overwriting it. Merging is the one thing that is never automa"},{"t":"Updating safely","p":"Everyday tools","u":"tools.html#updating","b":"Use dcli update or safe-update , not a bare pacman -Syu . Three layers guard it: A pacman PreTransaction hook ( pacman-preflight ) that refuses when / is too full. It fires for any tool, so it cannot be bypassed. A dcli pre_update hook that blocks on low space, snapshots stored on the root device, or an inconsistent package database. A post_update hook that finds AUR packages broken by a library soname bump and puts the rebuild command straight on your clipboard ( post-update-notify ). The recovery ladder , in order: downgrade → the LTS fallback kernel at the boot menu → the LTS rescue entry ("},{"t":"Every script","p":"Everyday tools","u":"tools.html#all","b":"A few worth knowing that are not covered above. Command What it does qtile-keys Print every documented keybinding, parsed from the live config's AST reset_PC Soft reset of the session — Super + Shift + F5 ui-scale , ui-scale-toggle HiDPI scaling boot-splash The boot splash wal-precompile Precompile a palette per wallpaper wal-audit Walk the wallpaper directory and report palette quality as CSV + a report battery-events , battery_notify Plug/unplug popups and the battery chip's popup. Both find the battery and adapter by type in /sys , never by name — BAT0 / AC is what one laptop happens to cal"},{"t":"Troubleshooting","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html","b":"Reorganised from TROUBLESHOOTING.md , which records 126 real cases as symptom → root cause → fix. The most likely ones are first."},{"t":"The first five minutes","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#first","b":""},{"t":"The install finished and I have a black screen with a blinking cursor","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#black-screen","b":"That is the install working. There is no display manager and no autologin, on purpose — the disable-dm module disables lightdm , gdm , sddm and lxdm . Log in with the username and password you chose. Type letsgo . If that says command not found , type startx . letsgo is a fish function , so it only exists when the account's login shell is fish. The login-shell module does that with chsh , but skips itself if fish is not installed. On such a machine startx is the one that works."},{"t":"startx says \"Server is already active for display 0\"","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#x-active","b":"An unclean X exit left /tmp/.X0-lock behind. letsgo removes it for you and tells you it did. By hand: pgrep -x Xorg # if this prints a PID, X really is running -- Ctrl+Alt+F1 rm -f /tmp/.X0-lock # only when it is not startx"},{"t":"The desktop did not appear after the first ATI-OS boot","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#desktop-missing","b":"The wizard runs on first boot, not during the install. It takes about 20 minutes and prints its progress on screen. If it failed: cat /var/log/ati-os-firstboot.log # the whole run cat /var/lib/ati-os/firstboot-complete # the wizard's exit status cd ~/.dotfiles/installScripts && ./wizard.sh The marker records the exit status rather than merely that the run happened, so a failed first boot does not mark itself done and will retry."},{"t":"validate.sh says everything passed but nothing works","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#validate-lies","b":"validate.sh degrades every check it cannot run to \"skipped\" — so on a machine where the install failed and nothing is present, it skips everything and exits green. On a fresh machine, trust the wizard's summary card ( 46 ok / 0 failed ) over validate.sh ."},{"t":"The desktop came up but it looks like stock qtile","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#stock-qtile","b":"qtile falls back to its built-in configuration when yours raises an error, and qtile cmd-obj -f status answers OK either way. Check the log for the fallback: grep -i \"config\" ~/.local/share/qtile/qtile.log | head bash ~/.dotfiles/installScripts/validate.sh # loads the config for real"},{"t":"The one rule","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#rule","b":"On a clean install, the module that reports the failure is almost never the module at fault. One full disk once presented as five broken modules, 32 modules after the cause. One blocked pacman transaction presented as two. Read the per-module logs ( /tmp/wizard-<id>.err ), not the wizard's summary card — that is what distinguished five root causes from twenty-two symptoms. The corollary, learned the same way: verify a check against a known-good case before trusting it. Five separate times in this repo's history the check was wrong rather than the code."},{"t":"Install and first boot","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#install","b":"The wizard finished but packages are missing dcli sync prints \"✓ Packages synced successfully\" regardless of what happened above it. This has shown up for three unrelated root causes. Always verify: dcli sync --dry-run # after any large sync, if a binary seems missing The three causes seen so far: The sudo credential cache expired mid-build. Packages show Finished making: X but never get pacman -U 'd. install.sh now keeps sudo alive for the whole run. A hard package conflict blocked the entire batch. It is one pacman -U transaction, so every package in that round fails, not just the conflictin"},{"t":"The ATI-OS medium","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#atios","b":"The machine will not boot the USB stick at all Three things, in this order: UEFI, not legacy/CSM. The boot menu usually shows each device twice; pick the entry that says UEFI. ATI-OS installs UEFI-only. Secure Boot off. ATI-OS ships an unsigned bootloader. The installer warns about this, but skip past it and the machine installs fine and then refuses to boot with \"no bootable device\" — which looks like a broken install and is not one. The write actually finished. dd returns before the data is on the stick unless you passed oflag=sync . Verify with sha256sum -c ati-os-*.iso.sha256 . \"no bootabl"},{"t":"The desktop","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#desktop","b":"Every app-launching keybind crashes with cmd_spawn Symptom: browser toggles, terminal, file manager, Telegram and Obsidian all fail with AttributeError: 'Qtile' object has no attribute 'cmd_spawn' in ~/.local/share/qtile/qtile.log . Cause: a pacman -Syu upgraded qtile itself, which removed Qtile.cmd_spawn() in favour of Qtile.spawn() — same signature, a drop-in rename. Fix: mechanical rename of every qtile.cmd_spawn( call site. Newly-installed packages or config do not take effect after reload_config Use Super + Shift + r (a full restart) rather than reload_config . The restart preserves windo"},{"t":"Theming","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#theming","b":"Everything renders in a CJK face and every aligned column is ragged Symptom: on a fresh install the bar, every popup and the notifications come up in a wrong-looking proportional face, and columns that were supposed to line up are ragged. Nothing errors and no log line mentions fonts. It reads like the layout code is broken. Cause: fontconfig never errors on a missing family. It substitutes, silently, and on this system fc-match answers Noto Sans CJK KR for anything it does not have. Every padded column in this repo is aligned with spaces , and spaces only align in a monospace font. fc-match \""},{"t":"Network and Bluetooth","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#network","b":"Fresh install: the WiFi/Bluetooth popups say the daemon is not running Symptom: Super + P then n or b opens the popup and immediately errors — NetworkManager is not running , or an empty Bluetooth list that never fills. nm-applet and blueman-applet are visible in the tray and do nothing. Cause: declaring a package installs a binary, not a running daemon. networkmanager and bluez are declared and installed, but for a long time nothing enabled the units. They were enabled on the development machine only because archinstall did it — exactly the kind of difference a package audit cannot see. ./wiz"},{"t":"The rofi menus","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#rofi","b":"Every confirmation dialog answers \"cancel\" by itself, on a second account On a machine with more than one user, one account's rofi state was in a fixed /tmp path owned by whoever got there first. All of those now route through ${XDG_RUNTIME_DIR:-/tmp/rofi-$(id -u)} . Pressing Enter in rofi_pass does nothing at all The picker opens, entries are listed, Enter closes it and nothing is copied. In the full file under Rofi , together with two other rbw cases: entries created with an empty password, and \"multiple entries found\" on get/remove/edit. Vaultwarden web vault 404s while the API works http:/"},{"t":"Voice dictation","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#dictation","b":"The mic transcribes garbage, or a session is suddenly much worse than the last one WirePlumber resets the mic capture gain to clipping levels on every login. The mic-gain module enables fix-mic-gain.service , which reasserts it. voice_dictate feels much slower than it should Transcribing a few seconds takes 10+ seconds when whisper-cli comes from the unoptimised AUR build. The whisper-fast module rebuilds it optimised and shadows the AUR binary via /usr/local — roughly 13× faster. It warns and skips rather than failing if it cannot, since it is a speedup, not a requirement. voice_dictate_live "},{"t":"Shell and session","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#shell","b":"A wall of errors on every new fish shell Unknown command: colorscript , mktemp: failed to create file via template '~/tmp/.psub.XXXXXX' , a Rust panic Broken pipe , source: missing filename argument . All covered under Fish shell in the full file. The image-envs module is what ensures ~/tmp exists. tmux ignores every plugin tmux starts fine and silently ignores every set -g @plugin line, so vim-tmux-navigator 's pane navigation and resurrect/continuum's save and restore all do nothing. TPM needs a bootstrap; that is the tmux-tpm module, and it runs as part of the install. Warnings on every qti"},{"t":"The full file","p":"Troubleshooting","u":"troubleshooting.html#full","b":"TROUBLESHOOTING.md is the complete record — 126 entries across these sections, each with symptom, root cause and fix: Section Covers Theming Palette propagation across all 15 consumers, fonts, popup rendering Rofi Every rofi script, plus rbw / Vaultwarden / Tailscale Qtile freezes Main-loop blocking Qtile config qdrop, widgets, popups, pcmanfm-qt, battery, Homerow Network & Bluetooth The WiFi and Bluetooth pickers and their daemons Voice dictation Mic gain, whisper speed, live-typing artefacts Testing on a fresh Arch VM How to reproduce a clean install Wizard / Installer Module failures, flags"},{"t":"Reference","p":"Reference","u":"reference.html","b":"Where everything lives, how it gets there, and the handful of things in this repository that read like bugs and are deliberate."},{"t":"Repository layout","p":"Reference","u":"reference.html#layout","b":"~/.dotfiles/ ├── .config/ everything that gets stowed into ~/.config │ ├── qtile/ the window manager: config.py, popups/, scripts/ │ ├── AtiScriptsV1/ ~50 commands, symlinked to /usr/local/bin │ ├── arch-config/ declarative package manifests (dcli) │ ├── eww/ the onboarding tour, tooltips, cheatsheet window │ ├── rofi/ shared base.rasi + per-menu themes │ ├── kitty/ dunst/ picom/ fish/ nvim/ qutebrowser/ ... │ └── secrets.env.example template for GEMINI_API_KEY ├── installScripts/ │ ├── install.sh = wizard.sh --yes │ ├── wizard.sh the 48 registered modules │ ├── stow_script.sh symlink deployme"},{"t":"How stow works here","p":"Reference","u":"reference.html#stow","b":"installScripts/stow_script.sh wraps GNU stow. It is run by the wizard's stow module, and it can be run by hand on any Linux — it is the one path that deploys the configuration half of this repo without pacman. bash ~/.dotfiles/installScripts/stow_script.sh Four things it does that plain stow . does not: It derives its own location. DOTFILES_DIR comes from where the script actually lives, not from an assumed ~/.dotfiles . The repo is routinely checked out elsewhere — a clone under a different name, a git worktree, /tmp during container tests — and hardcoding the path made it silently stow a dif"},{"t":"Where configuration lives","p":"Reference","u":"reference.html#config","b":"What Path The window manager ~/.config/qtile/config.py — the single largest file in the repo, and the only writable entry in the logo menu's Documentation section Popups ~/.config/qtile/popups/ qdrop, qupdate, the restart veil ~/.config/qtile/scripts/ Autostart ~/.config/qtile/autostart.sh Commands ~/.config/AtiScriptsV1/ , symlinked to /usr/local/bin X session ~/.xinitrc (written by the xinit module), ~/.Xresources (the xresources module) Secrets ~/.config/secrets.env , mode 600, gitignored, seeded from secrets.env.example Fonts ~/.config/fontconfig/fonts.conf — edit here to restyle the popup"},{"t":"Packages: dcli and arch-config","p":"Reference","u":"reference.html#packages","b":"Every pacman and AUR package is declared in YAML and reconciled by dcli . Change a module, run dcli sync , and the system converges. timeshift-autosnap snapshots on every sync, so a broken update is a one-command rollback. arch-config/ ├── config.yaml pointer to the active host ├── hosts/ati.yaml enabled_modules + host packages └── modules/*.yaml To add or remove a package: edit the right modules/*.yaml , run dcli sync , then commit once the machine is verified working. cd ~/.dotfiles git add .config/arch-config git commit -m \"arch-config: <change>\" host: means USERNAME, not hostname. It reads"},{"t":"The module files","p":"Reference","u":"reference.html#modules-yaml","b":"The host's enabled_modules list is what dcli sync installs. Three files are deliberately absent from it. File Contents In enabled_modules ? base.yaml Core system: base , base-devel , dcli itself, timeshift, pacman-contrib, the LTS rescue kernel Implicit base apps.yaml Daily applications — kitty, alacritty, brave-bin, firefox, qutebrowser, and the rest Yes wm.yaml qtile, qtile-extras, picom, qt5ct/qt6ct. qtile itself is declared explicitly, having previously only been pulled in as a qtile-extras dependency — an upstream packaging change could have silently removed the desktop Yes dev.yaml nvim,"},{"t":"Runtime state","p":"Reference","u":"reference.html#state","b":"All of it is generated. None of it is tracked, and none of it needs bootstrapping. Path What ~/.cache/qtile/theme_mode The active theme mode name ~/.cache/qtile/current_palette.json The 9-slot palette, rewritten on every apply ~/.cache/qtile/palettes/ Precompiled per-wallpaper palettes ~/.cache/qtile/ui_scale The per-machine HiDPI factor ~/.cache/qtile/layout_state.json MonadTall ratios per group, saved every 3 s ~/.cache/qtile/window_group_state.json window id → group, plus per-group focus order ~/.cache/qtile/gtk-wal.css The GTK overlay @import ed by gtk 3 and 4 ~/.cache/qtile/.theme-apply.l"},{"t":"The test layers","p":"Reference","u":"reference.html#tests","b":"Layer Time Catches validate.sh seconds Parses every tracked shell, Python, fish and YAML file, loads the qtile config for real , greps for hardcoded home paths, checks every .tmpl has a renderer, and runs a full wizard dry-run wizard.sh --audit seconds Declared packages vs installed, both directions container-test.sh ~3 min A real install as a user called tester : a path that only resolved because $HOME happened to be the author's own home directory, an unrendered @HOME@ template, a step that is not idempotent, a module YAML that no longer parses vm-test.sh --unattended ~2 h X11, systemd, boot"},{"t":"The repository's own documents","p":"Reference","u":"reference.html#docs","b":"File What it is for README.md The overview, the install, and the collapsible reference sections TROUBLESHOOTING.md 126 real cases, each symptom → root cause → fix HANDOFF.md The short brief for picking the repo up cold: what is verified and how, what must not be \"fixed\", and the repo-specific traps plan_found-but-not-fixed.md The long version. Everything an audit turned up and deliberately left alone, with the reason — including items that are correct as written THEME_AUDIT_REPORT.md The theming audit .config/qtile/WAL_PRECOMPILE_REPORT.md How the per-wallpaper palettes are forced to a doomone"},{"t":"Things that look like bugs and are not","p":"Reference","u":"reference.html#quirks","b":"Recorded in plan_found-but-not-fixed.md precisely so nobody re-breaks them. host: keys off the username, not the hostname. See above . grub_boost.sh refuses rather than guessing. It aborts on a trailing-comment or unquoted GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX_DEFAULT instead of corrupting it, and prints the offending line. wizard.sh hard-fails on non-Arch in preflight . Deliberate. Loosening the gate would let pacman-only steps run on a distro that cannot satisfy them. boot/*.conf hardcode a PARTUUID and intel-ucode.img . They are reference copies that are never installed; the wizard generates live entries from"}];
